+<div class="picks"><div><span>15</span><b>Wax beside bronze</b><span>the &#x27;made&#x27; paragraph in one true picture</span></div><div><span>13</span><b>Hannah&#x27;s own sketch</b><span>scanned flat, as it is, no ageing filter</span></div><div><span>24</span><b>A light study</b><span>one totem across a day, a 6–12 frame strip beside the lore</span></div><div><span>25</span><b>In the Vermont landscape</b><span>moss, bark, a creek stone, shot flat</span></div><div><span>21</span><b>Cyanotype with leaves</b><span>the totem&#x27;s own silhouette among pressed leaves</span></div></div>
+<section class="theme"><p class="kicker">A</p><h2>The museum record</h2><p class="lede">Plain, honest, documentary: the totem as if it already sat in a collection.</p><div class="wall"><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/01a.jpg" alt="Cat amulet, Egypt. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Cat amulet, Egypt. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="img/moodboard/01b.jpg" alt="Cat amulet, Egypt. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Cat amulet, Egypt. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="img/moodboard/own-grey.webp" alt="Rebi, grey backdrop. Studio Maçon" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Rebi, grey backdrop. Studio Maçon · ours</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">01</p><h3>Plain museum object shot</h3><p>One object on a neutral sweep, soft even light, front/side/back, nothing else.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Natural, and the grey-backdrop shots already do it. Cold on its own: the lore carries the myth.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Already have it</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/553799" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Met object page ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/02a.jpg" alt="Netsuke of a seated hare. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Netsuke of a seated hare. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="img/moodboard/02b.jpg" alt="Okimono of a raven. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Okimono of a raven. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">02</p><h3>Netsuke and okimono catalogues</h3><p>Japanese miniature carved animals shot close, in rotations, undersides shown.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Very strong: the same scale, subject and pocket-companion idea. The Hare with Amber Eyes gave netsuke a story of passing hand to hand.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy (turntable series)</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/59676" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Met netsuke ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/03.jpg" alt="Roman horse figurine, Portable Antiquities Scheme" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Roman horse figurine, Portable Antiquities Scheme · CC BY-SA 4.0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">03</p><h3>Small-find record</h3><p>Plain ground, a small scale bar, a find number.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> A scale bar says &#x27;2.8 cm&#x27; honestly. Styled, it becomes the relic sheet again.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/04.jpg" alt="Roman coin in raking light, PAS" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Roman coin in raking light, PAS · CC BY 2.0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">04</p><h3>Raking light / RTI</h3><p>One low light across the surface shows every carving mark; RTI lets you relight it.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Shows the wax-carved surface and backs up the &#x27;made&#x27; text. Could become a relightable viewer.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> One lamp (RTI: free method, more work)</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://culturalheritageimaging.org/Technologies/RTI/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cultural Heritage Imaging: RTI ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/05.jpg" alt="Lubbock, Prehistoric Times, fig. 103" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Lubbock, Prehistoric Times, fig. 103 · Public domain</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">05</p><h3>Archaeological line and stipple</h3><p>Pen drawings of finds in face, profile and section, dotted shading.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Close to what felt forced. Only works drawn loosely by Hannah, not styled as a document.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Hours per piece</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/06.jpg" alt="Pitt Rivers Museum interior" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Pitt Rivers Museum interior · CC BY-SA 4.0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">06</p><h3>Handwritten tags (Pitt Rivers)</h3><p>Dense cases, every small object with its own handwritten tag.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Take the tag, not the case: a manila tag on string, written by hand.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/07.jpg" alt="Ferrante Imperato&#x27;s cabinet, 1599" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Ferrante Imperato&#x27;s cabinet, 1599 · Public domain</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">07</p><h3>Cabinet of curiosities</h3><p>Imperato&#x27;s and Worm&#x27;s cabinets: shelves, drawers, crocodiles on the ceiling.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Mood, not a model. High risk of feeling forced.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> n/a</p></div></article></div></section><section class="theme"><p class="kicker">B</p><h2>Typologies and quiet still life</h2><p class="lede">The same light and scale, repeated, so the differences show.</p><div class="wall"><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/08.jpg" alt="Blossfeldt, Urformen der Kunst. Rijksmuseum" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Blossfeldt, Urformen der Kunst. Rijksmuseum · CC0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">08</p><h3>Typology grids</h3><p>Blossfeldt, the Bechers: identical framing, light and scale, many objects.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Very strong for collections: every animal at one scale on one ground. A scientist laying out leaves, like Hannah did.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Mostly have it</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/285454" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernd &amp; Hilla Becher, The Met ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/09.jpg" alt="Talbot, Articles of China. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Talbot, Articles of China. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">09</p><h3>Articles on shelves</h3><p>Talbot, 1844: modest things lined up on plain shelves, almost an inventory.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Very natural: a real plank, real objects, nothing staged.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy</p></div></article><article class="card textonly"><div class="words"><p class="num">10</p><h3>Morandi tonal still life</h3><p>A few objects close together, dusty muted tones, the gaps matter.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Good if the props are ordinary studio things (wax block, burr, stone), not &#x27;meaningful&#x27; props.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://www.fondazionemorandi.it" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fondazione Morandi ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card textonly"><div class="words"><p class="num">11</p><h3>Irving Penn close still life</h3><p>Found objects shot very sharp and large on plain paper.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Makes a 3 cm object monumental. Hero shots.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Macro lens</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://www.irvingpenn.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">irvingpenn.org ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card textonly"><div class="words"><p class="num">12</p><h3>Mingei catalogues</h3><p>Soft natural light, fabric or wood ground, no fuss, the made thing first.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Strong in spirit: the maker, use, humility.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_Folk_Crafts_Museum" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nihon Mingeikan ↗</a></p></div></article></div></section><section class="theme"><p class="kicker">C</p><h2>The hand and the making</h2><p class="lede">What really happened to the object: the sketch, the wax, the mould, the bench.</p><div class="wall"><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/13a.jpg" alt="Barye, Study of Cats, 1836. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Barye, Study of Cats, 1836. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="img/moodboard/13b.jpg" alt="Barye, Walking Tiger. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Barye, Walking Tiger. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="img/moodboard/own-sketch.jpg" alt="Rebi, charcoal. Studio Maçon" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Rebi, charcoal. Studio Maçon · ours</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">13</p><h3>Animalier sketches</h3><p>Barye&#x27;s pencil and watercolour studies, by the man who cast small bronze animals in 1840s Paris.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Excellent and true: Maçon&#x27;s real ancestor. Hannah&#x27;s own sketches fill this role directly.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Already have them</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/14a.jpg" alt="Trial piece, Akhenaten / horse. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Trial piece, Akhenaten / horse. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="img/moodboard/14b.jpg" alt="Sculptor&#x27;s model of a fist. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Sculptor&#x27;s model of a fist. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">14</p><h3>Trial pieces and models</h3><p>Egyptian sculptors&#x27; practice pieces: half-finished heads, a horse scratched on the back.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Precedent for showing the unfinished wax.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> n/a</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/15b.jpg" alt="Degas, Study of a Mustang, original wax. NGA" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Degas, Study of a Mustang, original wax. NGA · CC0</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="img/moodboard/15a.jpg" alt="Lost-wax apple model" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Lost-wax apple model · CC BY-SA 2.5</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">15</p><h3>The wax itself</h3><p>The carved wax before casting. Degas&#x27;s waxes were the work; the bronzes came later.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Top pick: unique to Maçon, true, unfakeable. Wax beside bronze tells the &#x27;made&#x27; paragraph in one picture.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Shoot each wax before the foundry</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/16.jpg" alt="Rubber moulds, lost wax" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Rubber moulds, lost wax · CC BY-SA 4.0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">16</p><h3>Moulds, sprues, the casting tree</h3><p>Rubber moulds, the wax tree, raw castings still on their sprues.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Strong and honest. A raw casting on its sprue is a striking image.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Ten minutes at the foundry</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/17.jpg" alt="Lomax, Bronze Statue Chasing Shop" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Lomax, Bronze Statue Chasing Shop · Public domain</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">17</p><h3>Foundry</h3><p>Pouring, glowing crucibles, leather aprons.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> One image, sparingly. Generic if overused.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> One visit</p></div></article><article class="card textonly"><div class="words"><p class="num">18</p><h3>Hands at the bench</h3><p>Tools, burrs, files, the gold head being brought up.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Natural; keep it plain, no shallow-focus romance.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://completedworks.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Completedworks ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/own-hand.webp" alt="Yael in hand. Studio Maçon" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Yael in hand. Studio Maçon · ours</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">19</p><h3>In the palm, for scale</h3><p>One totem in an open hand.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Essential at 2–4 cm, and already on every page.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Already have it</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://www.sophiebuhai.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sophie Buhai ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/20.jpg" alt="Steichen, Brâncuși&#x27;s studio, 1920" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Steichen, Brâncuși&#x27;s studio, 1920 · Public domain</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">20</p><h3>The sculptor&#x27;s studio</h3><p>Brâncuși photographed his own studio: work among plaster dust and plinths.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> A model for how to see: the work living among the tools. Shoot the bench as it is.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://www.tate.org.uk/visit/tate-st-ives/barbara-hepworth-museum-and-sculpture-garden" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hepworth studio, Tate ↗</a><a href="https://www.noguchi.org/isamu-noguchi/digital-features/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Noguchi Museum ↗</a></p></div></article></div></section><section class="theme"><p class="kicker">D</p><h2>Direct traces</h2><p class="lede">Contact, shadow, impression: marks the object left itself.</p><div class="wall"><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/21a.jpg" alt="Anna Atkins, British Algae. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Anna Atkins, British Algae. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="img/moodboard/21b.jpg" alt="Talbot, photogenic drawing. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Talbot, photogenic drawing. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">21</p><h3>Cyanotypes and photograms</h3><p>The object laid on light-sensitive paper, leaving its silhouette.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Excellent with Vermont leaves and stones: a real trace, not an illustration. Doubles as a packaging insert.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Cyanotype paper, an afternoon of sun</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/22.jpg" alt="Rubbing of a stele. The Met" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Rubbing of a stele. The Met · CC0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">22</p><h3>Rubbings</h3><p>Paper over a surface, rubbed with graphite or ink.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Totems are too small and round; works for ring bands or the crest.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/23a.jpg" alt="Bradbury fern nature print" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Bradbury fern nature print · Public domain</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">23</p><h3>Nature prints and herbaria</h3><p>Plants pressed into lead, or taped to card with a handwritten label.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Strong for the founder story; there&#x27;s even a Vermont herbarium.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Free</p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/24.jpg" alt="Contact prints, State Library of NSW" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Contact prints, State Library of NSW · Public domain</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">24</p><h3>Contact sheets</h3><p>The whole roll, misses and all, picks circled in grease pencil.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> &#x27;Never the same twice in the same light&#x27;: one totem across 12 frames as the light moves.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> A roll, or a phone burst</p></div></article></div></section><section class="theme"><p class="kicker">E</p><h2>Landscape, editorial, digital</h2><p class="lede">Where it lives once it leaves the studio.</p><div class="wall"><article class="card textonly"><div class="words"><p class="num">25</p><h3>Placed in the landscape</h3><p>Goldsworthy, Richard Long: small things left in their setting.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> The strongest emotional fit (tiny worlds from leaves and stones). Shoot it flat and documentary to avoid fairy-garden kitsch.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> A walk</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://www.goldsworthy.cc.gla.ac.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Goldsworthy archive ↗</a><a href="https://www.richardlong.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Long ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card textonly"><div class="words"><p class="num">26</p><h3>Editorial interiors</h3><p>The object on a real windowsill, desk or bedside, in natural light.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Good for &#x27;where it lives&#x27;.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Easy</p><p class="refs"><a href="https://www.apartamentomagazine.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apartamento ↗</a><a href="https://www.thegentlewoman.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Gentlewoman ↗</a><a href="https://www.aesop.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aesop ↗</a></p></div></article><article class="card"><figure><img src="img/moodboard/27.jpg" alt="Cosmic Buddha laser scan. Smithsonian" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Cosmic Buddha laser scan. Smithsonian · CC0</figcaption></figure><div class="words"><p class="num">27</p><h3>3D scan renders</h3><p>A turnable model, or a flat grey &#x27;clay&#x27; render.</p><p class="fit"><b>For Maçon</b> Useful as an interactive spinner; clinical as a still.</p><p class="effort"><b>Effort</b> Phone photogrammetry app</p></div></article></div></section>
